%YAML 1.1
%TAG !u! tag:unity3d.com,2011:
--- !u!114 &11400000
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 98395f251812ec84d8bbc703ba333038, type: 3}
  m_Name: Stage3_Quiz
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  quizId: Q3
  stageId: 3
  topics:
  - id: heat
    name: Urban heat island
  - id: air
    name: Air pollution
  - id: transit
    name: Transport & mass transit
  - id: burning
    name: Open burning
  - id: methane
    name: Landfills & methane
  - id: floods
    name: Flooding & esteros
  - id: game
    name: Game check
  preQuestions:
  - questionId: S3_PRE_1
    topicId: heat
    text: Why is Metro Manila often hotter than the countryside around it?
    choices:
    - Cities are closer to the sun because of their tall buildings
    - Concrete, asphalt and buildings absorb the sun's heat and release it slowly,
      and there are few trees to cool the air
    - Traffic noise warms the air
    - Rain never falls on large cities
    correctIndex: 1
    scored: 1
  - questionId: S3_PRE_2
    topicId: air
    text: What is the biggest source of air pollution in Metro Manila?
    choices:
    - Cooking fires in homes
    - Factories outside the city
    - Motor vehicles such as cars, buses, jeepneys and trucks
    - Ash from volcanoes
    correctIndex: 2
    scored: 1
  - questionId: S3_PRE_3
    topicId: transit
    text: Why does a full train usually produce less pollution per passenger than
      the same number of people travelling by car?
    choices:
    - Trains do not use any energy
    - Trains only run at night, when the air is cleaner
    - Train tracks absorb pollution from the air
    - One train carries hundreds of people at once, so each person's share of the
      emissions is much smaller
    correctIndex: 3
    scored: 1
  - questionId: S3_PRE_4
    topicId: burning
    text: Why is burning garbage in the open harmful?
    choices:
    - It releases toxic smoke and greenhouse gases, and Philippine law prohibits
      it
    - It is harmless as long as the pile is small
    - It makes garbage disappear without any pollution
    - It only harms plants, not people
    correctIndex: 0
    scored: 1
  - questionId: S3_PRE_5
    topicId: methane
    text: When food scraps and other organic waste rot in a dumpsite, which planet-warming
      gas do they release?
    choices:
    - Oxygen
    - Methane
    - Nitrogen
    - Hydrogen
    correctIndex: 1
    scored: 1
  - questionId: S3_PRE_6
    topicId: floods
    text: Why do many streets in Metro Manila flood quickly during heavy rain?
    choices:
    - Rain in the city is heavier than anywhere else in the country
    - The MRT tracks block the water
    - Garbage clogs the esteros and drains, so rainwater cannot flow away
    - Trees soak up too much water
    correctIndex: 2
    scored: 1
  postQuestions:
  - questionId: S3_POST_1
    topicId: heat
    text: A barangay replaces its small park with a concrete parking lot. What is
      most likely to happen to that area on sunny days?
    choices:
    - It becomes cooler because parked cars block the sun
    - Nothing changes, because the temperature depends only on the weather
    - It becomes hotter, because the concrete stores heat and there are no trees
      for shade
    - It gets more rain
    correctIndex: 2
    scored: 1
  - questionId: S3_POST_2
    topicId: air
    text: Which change would cut air pollution along EDSA the most?
    choices:
    - Fewer cars and old diesel vehicles on the road, with more people riding trains
      and buses
    - Putting up more billboards
    - Spraying water on the road every morning
    - Closing the parks near the highway
    correctIndex: 0
    scored: 1
  - questionId: S3_POST_3
    topicId: transit
    text: The MRT stops running for a day, and thousands of its riders take cars
      and taxis instead. What happens to emissions along EDSA?
    choices:
    - They drop, because the trains are switched off
    - They stay the same, because the same people are travelling
    - Only the noise changes, not the emissions
    - They rise, because many more vehicles are needed to carry the same number of
      people
    correctIndex: 3
    scored: 1
  - questionId: S3_POST_4
    topicId: burning
    text: A neighbour burns plastic and dry leaves in the backyard every week. What
      is the best advice?
    choices:
    - Burn them at night so nobody sees the smoke
    - Wet the pile first so the smoke is cleaner
    - Burn only the plastic and keep the leaves
    - Stop burning, because it releases toxic smoke and is prohibited; compost the
      leaves and segregate the plastic
    correctIndex: 3
    scored: 1
  - questionId: S3_POST_5
    topicId: methane
    text: What can a household do to cut the methane coming from dumpsites?
    choices:
    - Put all trash together in one plastic bag
    - Separate food and garden waste and compost it, so it does not rot in a dumpsite
    - Burn leftover food in the backyard
    - Throw food scraps into the nearest estero
    correctIndex: 1
    scored: 1
  - questionId: S3_POST_6
    topicId: floods
    text: A cleanup removes tons of garbage from an estero. What is the most likely
      effect during the next heavy rain?
    choices:
    - Rainwater drains away faster, so the flooding is less severe
    - The estero overflows even more
    - There is no effect on flooding
    - More typhoons will reach the city
    correctIndex: 0
    scored: 1
  - questionId: S3_GAME_1
    topicId: game
    text: In the Metro Manila stage, what do the enemies stand for?
    choices:
    - Wild animals that live in the city
    - City problems such as smog, traffic exhaust, garbage burning and landfills
    - Natural disasters such as earthquakes
    - Rival teams defending the city
    correctIndex: 1
    scored: 0
  - questionId: S3_GAME_2
    topicId: game
    text: In the boss level, why do Renewable allies keep their lanes safe from the
      Factory Titan's Furnace Slam?
    choices:
    - They stand for clean energy, which replaces the fossil-fuel power the Factory
      Titan stands for
    - Renewable allies have more health than other allies
    - The Factory Titan cannot see Renewable allies
    - Renewable allies make the level shorter
    correctIndex: 0
    scored: 0
